<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Justin I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 450–<a href="/years/527">527 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Justin I was a Byzantine emperor of the early sixth century, uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>. According to the site, a major Aksumite naval expedition across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> was undertaken at his request. It defeated the Himyarite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhu-nuwas">Dhu Nuwas</a> and extended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a>'s control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/justin-i.jpg" alt="Justin I" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solidus_of_Justin_I_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…c. 514–542 CE) marked the fullest extension of Aksumite reach: a major seaborne expedition around 525, undertaken with the encouragement of the Byzantine Emperor <b>Justin I</b>, overthrew the Himyarite king Dhu Nuwas, who had persecuted the Christians of Najran, and installed a Christian client ruler in South Arabia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The massacre provoked a response: encouraged by the Byzantine emperor <b>Justin I</b>, Kaleb led a large expedition across the Red Sea in 525, defeated the Himyarite army and killed Yusuf.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 520s King Gurgenes rose against Kavad I and appealed to the Byzantine Emperor <b>Justin I</b>, but when Persian armies invaded he fled with his family and nobles to Lazica, according to Procopius.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The ageing <b>Justin I</b> raised Justinian to co-emperor of the Eastern Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-1">1 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
